WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.089
You say mm-hm while a voice assistant is talking.

00:00:03.089 --> 00:00:06.541
Does it stop mid-sentence, or keep going, the way a person would?

00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:10.925
Hi, I'm Pippa!

00:00:10.925 --> 00:00:14.473
Haru is an open full-duplex speech model from a Japanese startup.

00:00:14.473 --> 00:00:17.698
Let's see how one model listens and talks at once.

00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:19.940
Chapter one.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.386
Why voice pipelines wait.

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:28.180
Most voice assistants are a relay of four models.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.738
First, a detector waits until you have been quiet long enough to

00:00:31.738 --> 00:00:32.784
be sure you finished.

00:00:33.204 --> 00:00:35.547
Then speech recognition writes down what you said.

00:00:35.547 --> 00:00:37.598
Your laugh and your tone stay behind.

00:00:38.018 --> 00:00:39.541
A text model writes the answer.

00:00:40.504 --> 00:00:42.532
And a speech synthesiser reads it out.

00:00:42.532 --> 00:00:44.560
Every stage waits for the one before.

00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:49.665
In a relay, only one side holds the floor, and a polite

00:00:49.665 --> 00:00:51.402
mm-hm looks exactly like an interruption.

00:00:51.822 --> 00:00:53.838
A full-duplex model drops the floor.

00:00:53.838 --> 00:00:57.870
It hears and speaks every frame, and overlap is just more data.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:00.357
Chapter two.

00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:01.888
Three streams, one clock.

00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:06.173
Here's the idea.

00:01:06.173 --> 00:01:10.135
Every frame, add your audio, the model's own audio, and its text

00:01:10.135 --> 00:01:12.776
into a single position of one language model.

00:01:14.742 --> 00:01:18.249
Time runs in frames of a hundred and thirty-three milliseconds.

00:01:18.249 --> 00:01:18.951
You ask.

00:01:18.951 --> 00:01:23.160
The model writes padding, then an end-of-padding token, and starts to speak.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.539
Take one frame.

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:29.017
Your audio, its own audio and its text token are added into one position.

00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:32.535
The output predicts the next text token and the next audio.

00:01:32.955 --> 00:01:34.926
Now you say mm-hm while it talks.

00:01:34.926 --> 00:01:36.898
That is just sound on your row.

00:01:36.898 --> 00:01:39.151
Trained on real overlapping speech, it keeps going.

00:01:39.571 --> 00:01:42.784
Interrupt it properly, and it should trail off a couple of frames

00:01:42.784 --> 00:01:45.729
in, the way people do in the recordings it learned from.

00:01:46.149 --> 00:01:46.957
Summing matters.

00:01:46.957 --> 00:01:49.783
Six frames interleaved would take eighteen positions.

00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:51.398
Summed, they take six.

00:01:53.258 --> 00:01:54.888
The company's own figure.

00:01:54.888 --> 00:02:00.594
Interleaving puts user audio, text and model audio in separate positions, one after another.

00:02:01.014 --> 00:02:01.988
Haru stacks them.

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:05.882
Three tokens share one position per frame, and the next text and

00:02:05.882 --> 00:02:07.180
audio come out together.

00:02:08.842 --> 00:02:09.536
Chapter three.

00:02:09.536 --> 00:02:10.923
Audio as one vector.

00:02:15.175 --> 00:02:19.250
Every frame, three thousand two hundred samples of your voice go through

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.306
the codec encoder and come out as sixty-four numbers.

00:02:22.306 --> 00:02:22.985
No codebook.

00:02:23.405 --> 00:02:26.925
Those numbers are added to the model's last text token and its

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:28.099
own last audio latent.

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:33.129
The sum is one position in the backbone, a four billion parameter language model.

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:34.536
Its output branches.

00:02:34.536 --> 00:02:36.838
A text head picks the next token.

00:02:36.838 --> 00:02:41.443
A small MeanFlow head turns noise into the next audio latent in one step.

00:02:42.883 --> 00:02:44.155
Compare Moshi-style codes.

00:02:44.155 --> 00:02:49.241
Each eighty millisecond frame is eight discrete codes, generated one after another

00:02:49.241 --> 00:02:50.937
by a second transformer.

00:02:51.357 --> 00:02:54.972
Haru's frame is a single latent, and the flow head runs once.

00:02:54.972 --> 00:02:57.383
Fewer frames per second, and one pass each.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:02.166
The head learns a mean flow, so one jump lands on the sample.

00:03:02.586 --> 00:03:04.941
Start from random noise, sixty-four numbers.

00:03:05.438 --> 00:03:09.203
Add the average velocity over the whole interval, which MeanFlow learns.

00:03:09.203 --> 00:03:10.572
One step is enough.

00:03:10.992 --> 00:03:14.096
It is conditioned on the backbone's hidden state for this frame.

00:03:14.516 --> 00:03:17.523
And guidance is an input, so it costs no second pass.

00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:19.810
Chapter four.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.293
How fast, and how true?

00:03:26.383 --> 00:03:27.581
Say your last syllable.

00:03:27.581 --> 00:03:29.377
It lands somewhere inside a frame.

00:03:29.797 --> 00:03:32.663
The model can't hear it until the frame completes.

00:03:32.663 --> 00:03:36.167
That's up to a hundred and thirty-three milliseconds, before any compute.

00:03:36.587 --> 00:03:37.946
Then one model step.

00:03:37.946 --> 00:03:41.684
On an M one Max with int8 weights, the company measures

00:03:41.684 --> 00:03:43.044
thirty-four point two milliseconds.

00:03:43.464 --> 00:03:49.337
With microphone, socket, output margin and headphones, the pipeline is sixty-five point two milliseconds.

00:03:49.337 --> 00:03:50.596
That's the headline.

00:03:51.016 --> 00:03:55.186
Add the framing back, and the worst case is about a hundred and ninety-eight.

00:03:55.186 --> 00:03:57.271
Level with Moshi's two hundred, not ahead.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.419
There is no turn-taking benchmark, but the demo videos keep each speaker

00:04:02.419 --> 00:04:03.626
on its own channel.

00:04:03.626 --> 00:04:07.849
Measured there, Haru takes the floor a fifth of a second after you stop.

00:04:09.425 --> 00:04:11.073
Here is the Japanese demo.

00:04:11.073 --> 00:04:15.027
Watch the playhead cross the mark for aizuchi, the short listening noises.

00:04:15.447 --> 00:04:17.357
Then a stretch of overlapping talk.

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:20.223
Both lanes busy at once, and neither one stops.

00:04:21.942 --> 00:04:23.851
The article found three soft spots.

00:04:24.271 --> 00:04:26.262
There is no benchmark at all.

00:04:26.262 --> 00:04:29.580
No error rate, no turn-taking test, no comparison with Moshi.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.338
The two-sided training audio was separated from single-track recordings, and separation is

00:04:35.338 --> 00:04:37.562
weakest exactly where people overlap.

00:04:37.982 --> 00:04:41.282
And the sixty-five milliseconds leaves out the frame you wait for.

00:04:42.683 --> 00:04:46.924
Sum both voices and the words into one position every frame, and

00:04:46.924 --> 00:04:49.044
listening becomes predicting your own silence.

00:04:50.817 --> 00:04:53.536
Three streams, one position, one step per frame.

00:04:53.536 --> 00:04:54.555
Thanks for watching!

00:04:55.417 --> 00:04:57.510
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.108
I'm Pippa.

00:04:58.108 --> 00:04:58.407
Bye!
